{
  "_doc": "Species book for the Tank — the 12 REAL templates guests colour (ids match v2/recognizer/species.py). The renderer reads ONLY this file to know how each recognized creature BEHAVES; the LOOK comes from the guest's own drawing PNG (served via /api/fish/manifest -> /fish/<id>.png), NOT from art in here. So there is deliberately no `count`, no `color`, no `texture` field: one Creature is spawned per real accepted fish and assigned its species profile by `id`. Adding a species = add an entry here (+ a template + recognizer row). No engine change.\n\nFIELD GUIDE:\n  role: prey|predator|neutral  -> drives pursue/evade forces.\n  scale: [min,max] sprite scale applied to the ~520px drawing (before depth falloff).\n  speed: [min,max] cruise speed px/s. max_force: steering cap. turn_rate: rad/s heading change cap.\n  depth_band: [near,far] in 0..1 parallax depth (0=front/largest/opaque, 1=back/smallest/faded). PURELY visual layering.\n  y_band: [top,bottom] fraction of tank height the creature prefers to occupy (0=surface,1=floor). A soft vertical force keeps it in band; this is how bottom-dwellers stay on the floor and jellyfish stay high. Omit for free-roaming.\n  y_band_force: strength of that vertical pull (higher = more strictly pinned).\n  schooling: true -> separation/alignment/cohesion with same species inside neighbor_radius (separation inside separation_radius). false -> loner (keeps personal space only).\n  weights: unitless multipliers on each active steering force: separation, alignment, cohesion, wander, drift_up, pursue, evade, flee. Absent key = behavior off.\n  flee_radius / pursue_radius: interaction distances (touch-flee, predator chase).\n  alpha: optional base opacity multiplier in 0..1 (before depth falloff). Jellyfish are slightly translucent so the scene shows through. Omit = fully opaque.\n  wobble: body animation style + amp + freq. Legacy SPRITE-TRANSFORM styles (still used by 11 species): undulate (lateral fish/shark), pulse (jellyfish bell), tail_wave (seahorse), paddle (turtle/manta flippers), scuttle (crab side-shuffle), idle (near-static urchin), startle (puffer inflate on flee). \n\n  MESH deformation (the new per-vertex foundation, meshbody.js): set wobble.mesh = a profile name and the renderer draws that species as a deformable PixiJS mesh instead of a transformed sprite, driven by a LIVE time uniform. Only the JELLYFISH uses it today; the rest keep the sprite wobble until tuned one at a time. Mesh grid = cols x rows verts over the texture. Per-vertex offset comes from the profile as a function of (u, v, t): v in [0=top,1=bottom] of the texture.\n    Jellyfish mesh params: pulseFreq (bell spring cycles/s), bellSplit (v boundary bell|tentacles), bellAmp (squash/stretch depth), bellWiden (sideways bulge on squash, volume trade), tentAmp (tentacle sway amplitude), waveFreq (tentacle ripple rate), waveSpeed (downward phase-travel of the wave), bobAmp (px of vertical bob synced to the bell power-stroke), bobDrift (px extra slow sink between strokes).\n\n  orient: how the drawing turns to face travel. 'level' = stays horizontal, faces left/right by mirroring on X (never a vertical flip), with a small clamped pitch (max_pitch_deg) — shark & most side-view fish. 'bank' = rotates fully to heading (top-down gliders: manta ray). 'upright' = never rotates (jellyfish, seahorse, crab, urchin), keeps its sway. max_pitch_deg: pitch clamp for 'level' fish.",
